GAPBS: perform the serialized-graph load copy in user space.

Why
---
GAPBS reads its CSR with file.read() (reader.h, ReadSerializedGraph), which is a
read(2) syscall: the kernel copies the bytes into the destination buffers. No
user-mode load or store executes for that copy, so Intel Pin cannot observe it.

M5's PAC and WAC sit at the memory controller and count it -- and a bulk load writes
every 64 B word of every destination page, the densest access pattern that exists.
For a kron-23 graph that is ~1.05 GB landing in ~269,000 4 KB pages that M5 records
as 64/64 words touched and a Pin-based tool records as nothing.

Measured directly (src/profiler/validate/kernel_blindspot.c), moving 512 MiB:

    via read(2):  5 DRAM accesses,          4 pages,  1.250 words/page
    via memcpy:   24,838,154 accesses, 262,150 pages, 63.999 words/page

Effect on the reproduction, BFS on kron-23 (P(page has <= N of 64 words touched)):

    N            4      8     16     32     48
    unpatched  .150   .289   .588   .807   .813
    patched    .063   .136   .265   .319   .320
    M5 Fig. 4  .050   .110   .170   .260   .345

The patch routes the copy through a 4 MB user-space staging buffer, so the writes to
the destination arrays become ordinary stores that Pin sees. The bytes that end up in
memory, and their layout, are identical; only which agent performs the final copy
changes. The staging buffer stays cache-resident and adds negligible traffic.

Use this to measure what M5's hardware would have seen. Leave it off to measure only
the application's own steady-state access pattern. Both are legitimate and answer
different questions; docs/methodology.md reports both.

Apply with:  git -C bench/gapbs apply ../../benchmarks/patches/gapbs-userspace-load.patch
diff --git a/src/reader.h b/src/reader.h
index 4544ba3..f5be7a3 100644
--- a/src/reader.h
+++ b/src/reader.h
@@ -10,6 +10,8 @@
 #include <string>
 #include <type_traits>
 
+#include <vector>
+#include <cstring>
 #include "pvector.h"
 #include "util.h"
 
@@ -36,6 +38,33 @@ class Reader {
   std::string filename_;
 
  public:
+
+  // Copy `bytes` from `in` into `dst` through a user-space staging buffer.
+  //
+  // file.read(dst, bytes) has the *kernel* write dst, which Intel Pin cannot observe:
+  // no user-mode load or store executes for a read(2) copy. A memory controller sees
+  // it, and it is the densest traffic there is -- every 64 B word of every
+  // destination page. Staging the data and memcpy-ing it makes those writes ordinary
+  // stores, so a memory-reference profiler sees what the controller would.
+  //
+  // Identical bytes and layout; only the agent doing the final copy differs. The 4 MB
+  // staging buffer stays cache-resident and adds negligible traffic of its own.
+  static void ReadUserspace(std::ifstream &in, char *dst, std::streamsize bytes) {
+    const std::streamsize kChunk = 4 << 20;
+    std::vector<char> staging(static_cast<size_t>(kChunk));
+    std::streamsize done = 0;
+    while (done < bytes) {
+      std::streamsize want = bytes - done;
+      if (want > kChunk) want = kChunk;
+      in.read(staging.data(), want);
+      std::streamsize got = in.gcount();
+      if (got <= 0) break;
+      memcpy(dst + done, staging.data(), static_cast<size_t>(got));
+      done += got;
+    }
+  }
+
+
   explicit Reader(std::string filename) : filename_(filename) {}
 
   std::string GetSuffix() {
@@ -287,13 +316,13 @@ class Reader {
     neighs = new DestID_[num_edges];
     std::streamsize num_index_bytes = (num_nodes+1) * sizeof(SGOffset);
     std::streamsize num_neigh_bytes = num_edges * sizeof(DestID_);
-    file.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(offsets.data()), num_index_bytes);
-    file.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(neighs), num_neigh_bytes);
+    ReadUserspace(file, reinterpret_cast<char*>(offsets.data()), num_index_bytes);
+    ReadUserspace(file, reinterpret_cast<char*>(neighs), num_neigh_bytes);
     index = CSRGraph<NodeID_, DestID_>::GenIndex(offsets, neighs);
     if (directed && invert) {
       inv_neighs = new DestID_[num_edges];
-      file.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(offsets.data()), num_index_bytes);
-      file.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(inv_neighs), num_neigh_bytes);
+      ReadUserspace(file, reinterpret_cast<char*>(offsets.data()), num_index_bytes);
+      ReadUserspace(file, reinterpret_cast<char*>(inv_neighs), num_neigh_bytes);
       inv_index = CSRGraph<NodeID_, DestID_>::GenIndex(offsets, inv_neighs);
     }
     file.close();
